chroot-ng is an original implementation: a freestanding (no libc) AArch64
runtime that emulates a chroot environment and bind mounts in-process, using a
seccomp-BPF SIGSYS monitor and ahead-of-time rewriting of `svc #0` sites instead
of ptrace. The path-containment resolver, the userland ELF loader, the syscall
monitor and its dispatcher, the link2symlink layer, the AF_UNIX and NETLINK_ROUTE
emulation, and the freestanding runtime are written from scratch.

Portions are ported from the sibling arm64chroot user-space emulator — among
them the guest-PID registry, the synthesized /proc files, the System V shared
memory / semaphore / message-queue layer, the guest ptrace model, and the
command-line help renderer. Both projects are authored by the same copyright
holder and licensed identically.

chroot-ng is functionally comparable to proot but shares no code with it; no
third-party source code is included or distributed with this project. The UAPI
constants and signal-frame layouts in include/cng/ are re-declared from the
stable AArch64 Linux syscall ABI rather than copied from kernel headers, which
is what keeps the freestanding build hermetic.

qemu-aarch64 is used only as a differential-testing oracle (see tests/); it is
neither included nor linked.
